    "message": {
     "content": "This is a **standard baseline initial offer** for a Google L6 Product Manager in the Bay Area. It is not a bad offer, but given that you are currently an IC6 at Meta making ~$526k/year, **it is not compelling enough to accept as-is**. \n\nBecause you are happy where you are and they recruited you, you hold maximum leverage. You should **negotiate assertively**.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Financial Breakdown & Reality Check\n\n| Component | Meta (Current IC6) | Google Offer (Initial L6) | Delta |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | **-$5,000** *(pay cut)* |\n| **Target Bonus** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | **+$11,000** |\n| **Annualized RSU** | ~$250,000 | $270,000 ($1.08M / 4 yrs) | **+$20,000** |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | N/A | $0 | **$0** |\n| **Year 1 Target TC** | **~$526,000** | **~$552,000** | **+$26,000 (+5%)** |\n\n#### Deconstructing the Recruiter's Pitch\n* **The \"Stacking Refresher\" Pitch:** The recruiter emphasized that annual refreshers stack over time to grow your comp. **Meta does the exact same thing**\u2014Meta awards annual refresh grants that vest quarterly over 4 years. Pointing to standard performance refreshes to justify a lateral entry package is recruiter misdirection.\n* **Base Pay:** Moving to Google should not involve a reduction in base salary, especially when the posted range goes up to $271,000.\n* **No Sign-on Bonus:** Google almost always has sign-on budget available at L6 to offset forfeited bonus timing or unvested equity.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Should You Accept, Negotiate, or Walk Away?\n\n* **Do not accept as-is.** A 5% increase in total comp does not justify resetting your tenure, team capital, and performance track record at Meta.\n* **Do not walk away immediately.** Google expects negotiation at L6, and making a data-driven counteroffer carries virtually zero risk of an offer being rescinded.\n* **Negotiate firmly.** Target a package that delivers a clear 15\u201320% premium over your current Meta comp.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Exactly What to Ask For (Your Counteroffer)\n\nGoogle L6 PM total compensation typically spans **$500k to $650k+** in the Bay Area for strong hires with competing leverage.\n\n| Component | Initial Offer | Counter Target | Bottom-Line Walk Number |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000** | $245,000+ *(must beat Meta)* |\n| **4-Year RSU** | $1,080,000 ($270k/yr) | **$1,320,000** ($330k/yr) | $1,200,000 ($300k/yr) |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000** | $40,000 \u2013 $50,000 |\n| **Target Year 1 TC** | **$552,000** | **~$711,000** *(incl. sign-on)* | **~$635,000+** |\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Structure the Conversation\n\nSchedule a call with your recruiter and deliver a calm, collaborative message emphasizing that you need the financial upside to justify leaving a role where you are thriving:\n\n> *\"Thank you again for the offer\u2014I really enjoyed meeting the team, and the scope of the L6 role aligns well with what I'm looking for. However, as we discussed early on, I wasn't actively looking and I'm in a very strong spot at Meta, where my annualized compensation is already around $526k, with $200k in equity vesting over the next 12 months alone.*\n>\n> *At $552k total comp with a slight decrease in base salary and no sign-on, this offer is largely lateral to my current situation once you account for the risk and tenure reset.*\n>\n> *If we can bridge the gap with the following adjustments, I would be excited to sign right away:*\n> 1. **Base Salary:** *$255,000 (to reflect my current baseline and stay competitive within your posted band)*\n> 2. **Equity:** *$1,320,000 over 4 years*\n> 3. **Sign-on Bonus:** *$75,000 to bridge the transition and forfeited Meta incentives*\n>\n> *Is this something you can take to the compensation committee?\"*\n\n---\n\n### 5. Key Due Diligence Item: Vesting Schedule\nAsk the recruiter to clarify the **exact GSU vesting schedule** on your offer letter. \n* Many Google new-hire grants follow a front-loaded schedule (e.g., **38% Year 1 / 32% Year 2 / 20% Year 3 / 10% Year 4**) rather than an even 25%/year.\n* If your grant is front-loaded, a $1.08M grant yields ~$410k in stock in Year 1, but drops sharply in Years 3 and 4. Understanding the exact vesting breakdown will ensure your cash-flow comparisons are precise.",
