    "message": {
     "content": "Getting a **$200,000 cash sign-on bonus** at Google L6 is an exceptional win. That is at the absolute upper ceiling of what Google approves for sign-on exceptions at this level without a higher written counter-offer.\n\nFinancially, **this is a top-of-market package for L6 Product Management**. \n\n---\n\n### 1. The Numbers: Before vs. After\n\n| Component | Meta (Current IC6) | Google Revised (Final L6) | Delta |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $240,000 | Match ($0) |\n| **Target Bonus** | $36,000 (15%) | $48,000 (20%) | **+$12,000** |\n| **Annualized RSU** | ~$250,000 | $270,000 ($1.08M / 4 yrs)* | **+$20,000** |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$200,000** | **+$200,000** |\n| **Year 1 Target TC** | **~$526,000** | **~$758,000 \u2013 $844,000+** | **+$232,000 to +$318,000** |\n\n*\\*Note on Year 1 Equity:* If your grant uses Google\u2019s front-loaded vesting (e.g., 33% in Year 1 = ~$356k), your **Year 1 total cash + stock will exceed $840,000**.\n\n#### 4-Year Guaranteed / Target Value:\n* **Meta (steady state):** ~$2.10M across 4 years.\n* **Google (new package):** **~$2.43M** across 4 years (an average of **~$608,000/year**, not including future performance refreshes).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Can You Push Any Further?\n**No.** When Google comes back with a base match and a $200k lump-sum sign-on stating it is their \"best and final,\" they have exhausted standard compensation committee discretion for L6. Pushing further without a competing offer in hand risks stalling the process or annoying the hiring director.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Should You Take It?\n\nBecause compensation matters significantly to you, **accepting is the rational financial choice**, but consider these final trade-offs:\n\n#### Why You Should Accept:\n1. **Upfront Liquidity & De-risking:** You replace your unvested $200k Meta RSUs with **$200,000 in immediate cash**, eliminating stock-price risk on that portion of your earnings.\n2. **Pedigree & Diversity of Experience:** Holding Senior PM (L6 / IC6) titles at both Meta and Google creates long-term career optionality and establishes you at the highest compensation tiers for future Director/VP roles.\n3. **Culture / WLB:** If work-life balance or a change of pace appeals to you, Google PM culture is generally less intense on performance stack-ranking and crunch than Meta.\n\n#### Reasons You Might Stay at Meta:\n1. **You Are Genuinely Thriving:** If you have deep political capital, a high-visibility project, and are on track for IC7 (Director/Principal level) in the next 12\u201318 months at Meta, staying could make sense. Reaching IC7 at Meta will jump your recurring total compensation to $800k\u2013$1M+/yr faster than reaching L7 at Google, where promotions tend to be slower and more consensus-driven.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Final Due Diligence Before Signing\n\nBefore returning the signed offer, confirm these two administrative details with the recruiter:\n1. **Sign-on Clawback Terms:** Check whether the $200k has a 1-year or 2-year prorated repayment clause if you leave voluntarily. (1 year is standard; if it is 2 years, clarify how it prorates).\n2. **Vesting Schedule:** Ask whether the $1.08M GSU grant vests **flat (25% per year)** or **front-loaded (e.g., 33%/33%/22%/12%)**, so you can plan your quarterly tax and cash flow.",
